Z||000003||BBBAD GT||07-03-2000||03:54 PM||||Haze:<P>Here is the quote from your web site:<P>Fact: The best 1/4- mile timef or a stock V6 Fiero is 14.7 @ 92. Thats faster than the stock 5.0L Mustang<P>Where did you get this info?  In all of the rags that I've ever read about the Fiero say they've done best a mid to low 15 sec 1/4 and around 85-88 mph.  92 mph is damn good for a full weight stocker.  Wish mine did that.<P><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||07-03-2000||06:40 PM||||  Here you go Haze!<BR> <BR>  2000 Mustang Spoiler on Fiero Notchback<P>  1.) The most difficult part of this mod is deciding how far forward to set the spoiler. For the sake of argument I wanted it mounted as far back as practicle, with the trailing edge of the spoiler mounting surface about 1.5" forward of the reardecklids downward roll. It is imperative that all measurements are taken from straight refference using the same measuring strategy (if you burn an inch, be sure to add it back).<BR> 2.) Using the decklid as a refference just to the rear of the vents, measure back (towards the rear) 12.25" on both sides. Next, measure 6.75" beyond the previous mark and make mark #2 . You should end up with two marks at 12.25" and 19" respectively on each side of the decklid.<BR> 3.) Find the center line of the decklid at two or three different places and connect these marks with a vertical line. Measuring out from center make a mark at 25.5" on either side of center at the 12.5" off line (do this on both sides. Next, Measure out from center 25.25" make a mark on the 19" off line. You should now have four marks; two at 12.5"x 25.5" and two at 19"x 25.25"<BR> These are the points where you will drill.<BR>Be sure to check for square before any drilling (you don't want a swiss cheese decklid).<BR> 4.) using a .25" drill bit, drill through the decklid. CAUTION: ensure decklid is proped open or damage may occur to the inner trunk seal. <BR> 5.)Using a 1" hole saw (finest cut you can find) start from the inside surface of the decklid (using the .25" hole as a guide) Cut through the inner surface ONLY!!! You will know when your are through. Proceed slowly and wear eye protection and a dust mask. You are cutting fiberglass and you niether want to inhale it or get it in your eyes.<BR> 6.)The studs on the spoiler will protrude throught the decklid and the 1" hole will allow ample room to afix the hardwear. Iwould recomend the use of a gasket on the outer mounting surface, or you might want to have the spoiler glassed in before painting. If it is your choice to use a gasket, I recomend using a heavy duty mouse pad, yes a mouse pad, like the ones used on computers. Trace the outline of the spoiler mounting surface on the pad or template and cut it out. This material is easily compressed yet flexible enough to allow the neccessary take up on the inner edge. Also recomended is the use of weather tight washers on the inner decklid surface. This ensures that moisture will not leak into the rear compartment. tighten down your hardware and you are ready to go. I would recomend that the spoiler be drilled and premounted prior to paint. Then it's as simple as removing the spoiler, having it painted, and re-installing it. Using a high speed drill and working cautiously, should eleminate the need for any paint work to the decklid, unless it is decided to glass the spoiler on. <BR>  Good luck and I'm sure you will enjoy the look of this mod. If there are any further questions you can feel free to E-Mail me at Bartlettds@lvcm.com I will be glad to answer any questions or clear up any uncertainty.<P> Thanks, D. Bartlett||||reg||6
Z||000005||Haze_Performance||07-04-2000||02:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>Haze:<P>Here is the quote from your web site:<P>Fact: The best 1/4- mile timef or a stock V6 Fiero is 14.7 @ 92. Thats faster than the stock 5.0L Mustang<P>Where did you get this info?  In all of the rags that I've ever read about the Fiero say they've done best a mid to low 15 sec 1/4 and around 85-88 mph.  92 mph is damn good for a full weight stocker.  Wish mine did that.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That was out of the pages of "Northern Illinois Fiero Enthusiasts, Fiero Focus" May/June 2000 issue.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Carl (85 Fiero) My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>Haze Mustang Performance</A>   &  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A>||||reg||1
